<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pancasila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pancasila is the official philosophy of the Indonesian state, set out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> in a speech of 1 June 1945. Its five principles are belief in one God, just and civilised humanity, the unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, democracy guided by deliberation and consensus, and social justice for all Indonesians. It is written into the preamble of the 1945 constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pancasila.jpg" alt="Pancasila" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Indonesia (uncredited) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garuda_Pancasila_Poster_(color).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1942 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> and later promised future self-government. On 1 June 1945, the last day of the first session of the Investigating Committee for Preparatory Work for Independence, the nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> proposed five principles as the philosophical basis of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. He named them Pancasila, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> pañca, five, and śīla, principles, and rejected the earlier name Panca Dharma because dharma means obligation. His original five were Indonesian nationalism, internationalism, deliberative consensus, social welfare, and a divinity that upholds religious freedom. The formulation was a compromise between advocates of an Islamic state and of a secular state.</p><p>A Committee of Nine drafted a preamble, the Jakarta Charter, and moved the principle of divinity to first place. Its first sila originally obliged its Muslim adherents to carry out Islamic law. On 18 August 1945 the Preparatory Committee for Indonesian Independence deleted that clause, leaving belief in the One and Only God, to ensure national unity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The five principles are belief in the One and Only God, just and civilised humanity, the unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, democracy led by wisdom in deliberation among representatives of the people, and social justice for all the people of Indonesia. Their legal formulation is in the fourth paragraph of the preamble of the constitution. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> suggested that the five could be compressed into three, socio-nationalism, socio-democracy and belief in God, and further into one, gotong royong, or mutual cooperation. The national motto, Bhinneka Tunggal Ika, often translated as unity in diversity, belongs to the same effort to unite a diverse archipelago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the Constitutional Assembly elected in 1955 the Pancasila Bloc held 53.3 per cent of the seats and the Islamic Bloc 44.8 per cent, and the debate could not be resolved. On 5 July 1959 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> dissolved the assembly by decree and reimposed the 1945 Constitution. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>'s New Order Pancasila was promoted as the supreme national ideology, with a mandatory indoctrination programme from 1978, and in 1983 adherence was made mandatory for all organisations. Critics say the New Order used its vagueness to legitimise authoritarian acts. After 1998 the coercive apparatus was dismantled, while Pancasila remained the foundational state ideology. Some scholars criticise it as overly vague; defenders argue that its generality gives the flexibility needed to manage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s diversity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pancasila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The next day a constitution was adopted that made Sukarno president and Hatta vice-president, with the state philosophy of <b>Pancasila</b> as its foundation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As defeat neared, Japan allowed a committee to prepare independence, and Sukarno set out the <b>Pancasila</b> principles in June 1945.</blockquote><a href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-the-dutch-east-indies">Japanese occupation of the Dutch East Indies</a></li></ul></section>
